We are asked: "Find the sum of all integer bases $b>9$ for which $17_b$ is a divisor of $97_b.$" So we need to interpret numbers in base b. 17_b means 1*b + 7 = b+7. 97_b means 9*b + 7 = 9b+7. So condition: (b+7) divides (9b+7). Since b>9, b is integer base >9. So we need integer b>9 such that (b+7) | (9b+7). Compute (9b+7) mod (b+7). We can do polynomial division: 9b+7 divided by b+7. 9b+7 = 9(b+7) - 63 + 7? Actually 9(b+7)=9b+63, so 9b+7 = 9(b+7) - 56. So remainder is -56, i.e., (9b+7) = 9(b+7) - 56. So (b+7) divides (9b+7) iff (b+7) divides 56. Because if (b+7) | (9b+7) and (b+7) | 9(b+7), then (b+7) divides their difference: (9(b+7) - (9b+7)) = 56? Actually careful: (9b+7) = 9(b+7) - 56. So (b+7) divides (9b+7) iff (b+7) divides 56. Since (b+7) divides 9(b+7) trivially, then (b+7) divides (9(b+7) - (9b+7)) = 56. So condition: (b+7) | 56. And b>9, so b+7 > 16. So we need divisors of 56 that are greater than 16. Divisors of 56: 1,2,4,7,8,14,28,56. Those >16: 28 and 56. So b+7 = 28 => b=21; b+7=56 => b=49. Also check if b+7 could be 56? Yes. Also b+7 could be 56? That gives b=49. Also b+7 could be 56? Yes. Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be 56? Also b+7 could be