ically. So N(x) would have r and t as simple roots. Then N(x) is quartic, so it has two more roots. Those could be real or complex. For f to have exactly three critical points (two minima and one maximum), we need N to have three positive roots. That would mean besides r and t, there is another positive root s (the maximum). And the fourth root would be negative. So in the perfect square scenario, we have r and t as two critical points (minima). Then there should be a third critical point (maximum) between them. That would correspond to another root of N. So N(x) would have roots r, s, t, and a negative root. So the perfect square condition on Q(x) does not directly give that N has only those roots; it gives that r and t are critical points with equal f value. But we also need that there is a maximum between them. That will happen if the quartic N has three positive roots. So we need to ensure that N(x) has three positive roots, and that the two minima have equal value. The perfect square condition ensures that the minima have equal value (since they both give f=m). But is it necessary? If the two minima have equal value m, then at those points, P(x)=m x and P'(x)=m, so those points are double roots of P(x)-m x. So indeed P(x)-m x has double roots at those points. Since it's a quartic, it can have at most two double roots (if it has two double roots, it's a perfect square). But could it have a double root and two simple roots? That would give one minimum and another point? Actually if there is a double root, that's a critical point with value m. If there is another distinct point with f=m but not critical? That would be a simple root of P(x)-m x, but then f=m at that point but derivative not zero. That could happen, but then the minimum value m is attained at a point that is not a critical point? But since f is differentiable, if it attains a minimum at an interior point, derivative must be zero (or undefined). So any point where the global minimum is attained must 